WEBVTT

00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:15.807
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> It’s crammed with around 55,000 objects on display,

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:23.418
ranging from prehistoric hand axes and a witch in a bottle, to a mask with movable eyes,

00:00:23.818 --> 00:00:25.326
and a leaf-shaped dagger.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.467
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Representatives from Haida Gwaii, a group of islands between Canada and Alaska,

00:00:39.073 --> 00:00:42.401
have been in conversation with both the Pitt Rivers in Oxford

00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:48.424
and the British Museum in London to improve relationships between the Haida people

00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:51.059
and the museums holding their treasures.

00:00:51.938 --> 00:00:58.083
For over a century, Haida artefacts have formed an important part of the Pitt Rivers collection.

00:00:59.043 --> 00:01:03.739
This includes this Haida war helmet, with its carved and painted octopus,

00:01:03.886 --> 00:01:09.413
which the Haida believe was worn by prominent chiefs during battle or ceremonial war dances.

00:01:16.911 --> 00:01:20.677
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> All of these items still have huge cultural significance to the local people,

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:26.783
something Haida artists Jaalen and Gwaii Edenshaw felt immediately

00:01:27.016 --> 00:01:30.371
when they first visited the Pitt Rivers in 2009.

00:01:31.618 --> 00:01:39.900
<v><b>Gwaii:</b></v> I didn’t expect it actually, but when I walked into the room with the chief’s headdresses,

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:46.640
it was a very emotional experience for me and, er, it caught me off guard.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.778
You know, those pieces felt like they were…lost.

00:02:01.104 --> 00:02:05.587
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> The importance of these items isn’t limited to the objects themselves;

00:02:06.374 --> 00:02:09.664
they tell us a huge amount about the cultures they come from.

00:02:11.377 --> 00:02:17.298
Take ‘The Great Box’, for example, part of the Pitt Rivers collection since 1874.

00:02:17.831 --> 00:02:19.959
We don’t know exactly what it was used for,

00:02:20.338 --> 00:02:24.980
but we do know it is a masterpiece of Haida Nation craftsmanship.